@* One Ability or Talent: name, a stepper, and pips showing how the rank divides between
   what a package granted and what was bought on top. A package buys the ranks it grants,
   so those pips are already paid for — CostCalculator only charges for the rest. *@

@inject Sliders Sliders

<div class="rank-row">
    @* What this Trait is, on hover and on focus.

       **The name is the trigger and it is a real button**, unlike the option rows, where the row
       itself is already one. A `<span>` with a mouse handler is a tooltip only for people using a
       mouse, and Abilities and Talents are the one surface in the app where a reader most often
       does not know what the word means — Presence and Willpower are not self-explanatory, and
       nothing on this row has ever said.

       It is not a `title` attribute, for the reasons the app already records: never on a touch
       screen, unstyleable, undismissable, announced inconsistently. *@
    <div class="rank-name">
        @if (Description is not null)
        {
            <button type="button" class="trait-term"
                    aria-describedby="@DescriptionId"
                    @onkeydown="DismissKey"
                    @onmouseleave="Restore"
                    @onblur="Restore">@Name</button>
            <span id="@DescriptionId" class="sr-only">@Description</span>
            <span class="row-tip @(_dismissed ? "dismissed" : "")" role="tooltip" aria-hidden="true">@Description</span>
        }
        else
        {
            @Name
        }
        @if (Sub is not null)
        {
            <small>@Sub</small>
        }
    </div>

    @* **The pips are the control, not a picture of one.** They were `aria-hidden` decoration
       beside the stepper, which meant the only way from 2d to 9d was seven clicks on `+`.
       Clicking the fifth pip sets 5d; the arrow keys move by one; Home and End go to the ends.

       `role="slider"` rather than a radio group: a rank is a value on a range, the range is
       bounded and ordered, and one focusable element beats twelve. The stepper stays — it is
       discoverable, it is a bigger touch target, and a slider and its buttons are an ordinary
       pairing.

       **The individual pips stay `aria-hidden`.** They are the slider's own rendering, and a
       reader told "4d Noteworthy, slider" does not also want twelve unlabelled children.

       **The row is drawn to `Ceiling`, not to `Max`** — see the doc comment on it. A cap tighter
       than what the character already has, or than what a package grants, is a finding the
       validator reports on this very row; it is not this component's to hide or to repair. *@
    <div class="pips"
         @ref="_pips"
         role="slider"
         tabindex="0"
         aria-label="@($"{Name} rank")"
         aria-valuemin="@Min"
         aria-valuemax="@Ceiling"
         aria-valuenow="@Rank"
         aria-valuetext="@ValueText"
         @onkeydown="Key">
        @for (var i = 1; i <= Ceiling; i++)
        {
            var rank = i;
            var cls = i <= PackageFloor ? "pip on package" : i <= Rank ? "pip on" : "pip";
            <span class="@cls" aria-hidden="true" @onclick="() => Set(rank)"></span>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="stepper">
        <button type="button" aria-label="@($"Lower {Name}")"
                disabled="@(Rank <= Floor)"
                @onclick="() => Set(Rank - 1)">−</button>
        <span class="value">@(Rank)d</span>
        <button type="button" aria-label="@($"Raise {Name}")"
                disabled="@(Rank >= Ceiling)"
                @onclick="() => Set(Rank + 1)">+</button>
    </div>

    @* The rulebook's word for this rank. Outside the stepper, because it is a gloss on the
       number rather than one of its controls, and it is left as an empty cell above 6d so
       the ranks below stay in a column. *@
    <div class="rank-word">@Word</div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = "";
    [Parameter] public string? Sub { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What this Trait is, in the rules' own terms, shown on hover and on focus.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Supplementary and never the only place something is said</b> — WCAG is explicit
    /// that information carried only by a tooltip is information some readers do not get. The row
    /// still prints the name, the rank and the rulebook's word for it; this adds what the Trait
    /// covers, which nothing on the row has ever said.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Description { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether Escape has closed the description for now. See <c>OptionRow</c>.</summary>
    private bool _dismissed;

    private void DismissKey(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape") _dismissed = true;
    }

    private void Restore() => _dismissed = false;

    /// <summary>
    /// Derived from the Trait's name rather than generated, so two renders of one character
    /// produce identical markup — the replay guard requires exactly that.
    /// </summary>
    private string? DescriptionId => Description is null
        ? null
        : "trait-" + new string(
            Name.ToLowerInvariant().Select(c => char.IsLetterOrDigit(c) ? c : '-').ToArray());

    [Parameter] public int Rank { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int Max { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The lowest rank allowed at all: 1d for both. Ch.2 states it once for Abilities (p.17)
    /// and again for Talents (p.18) — "No Ability can have a rank lower than 1d", "No Talent can
    /// have a rank lower than 1d". The Talents tab used 0d, which the book contradicts.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Floor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The rank a starting package already grants, and so pays for.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int PackageFloor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Ch.2's word for each rank of this Trait — the ABILITY RANKS table on printed p.17
    /// (Impaired, Undeveloped, Developed, Noteworthy, Exceptional, Peak) and the TALENT RANKS
    /// table on p.18 (Clueless, Unskilled, Proficient, Advanced, Expert, Master).
    ///
    /// <para>Passed in rather than looked up here, so this component still knows nothing about
    /// the rules; the tabs hand over <c>AbilityModel.RankGuide</c> or
    /// <c>TalentModel.RankGuide</c>, which have carried this the whole time with nothing
    /// showing them.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string>? RankGuide { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<int> RankChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The word for the rank as it stands, or nothing.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Both tables stop at 6d, and above that there is deliberately no word.</b> The
    /// rulebook stops because a 7d Trait is beyond what the scale describes — so a rank with
    /// no entry shows an empty cell rather than the nearest word, which would be this program
    /// inventing a rung the book does not have.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>The stored value carries a gloss on two of the rungs — "Undeveloped (ordinary
    /// human average)" — and only the word is shown here. The gloss is a sentence about
    /// people in general and this is a label on one row of a form.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? Word
    {
        get
        {
            if (RankGuide is null || !RankGuide.TryGetValue($"{Rank}d", out var entry)) return null;

            var gloss = entry.IndexOf(" (", StringComparison.Ordinal);
            return gloss < 0 ? entry : entry[..gloss];
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The lowest rank this row can actually be set to: the Trait's own floor, or the package's
    /// granted rank where that is higher, since a package's ranks "cannot be lowered below the
    /// package rank". The same figure <see cref="Set"/> clamps to, read once so the announced
    /// minimum and the enforced one cannot disagree.
    /// </summary>
    private int Min => Math.Max(Floor, PackageFloor);

    /// <summary>
    /// <b>The highest rank this row draws and can be set to.</b> Normally
    /// <see cref="Max"/> — the Trait Cap in force — but never below <see cref="Min"/> and never
    /// below the rank the character already carries.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The first half stops this component throwing.</b> A house Trait Cap is a number a
    /// GM types, and a campaign capped at 2d over a package that grants 3d makes
    /// <c>Min &gt; Max</c> — at which point <c>Math.Clamp</c> throws
    /// <c>ArgumentException</c> on the first pip click, taking the whole step down. A cap under a
    /// package floor is a character the validator already reports
    /// (<c>TRAIT_CAP_BELOW_MINIMUM</c>, <c>TRAIT_ABOVE_CAP</c>), and a row that crashes reports
    /// nothing at all.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>The second half keeps the ARIA honest.</b> A character built to 8d whose table
    /// then caps at 6d used to render six pips with <c>aria-valuenow="8"</c> against
    /// <c>aria-valuemax="6"</c> — a <c>slider</c> announcing a value outside its own range, which
    /// is the same fault the budget strip records for <c>progressbar</c>. The eight pips are drawn
    /// plainly, in the classes every other row uses; there is no "over the cap" ink and inventing
    /// one here would be a second way of saying what the finding beneath the row already says.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Reported, never repaired — and it ratchets rather than sticking.</b> Nothing here
    /// lowers the rank: the ceiling gives way to what is already on the sheet. Stepping 8d down to
    /// 7d moves the ceiling to 7d with it, so an over-cap Trait can come down and cannot climb
    /// again, which is the honest reading of a cap that is in force.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private int Ceiling => Math.Max(Math.Max(Max, Min), Rank);

    /// <summary>
    /// What a screen reader is told the value is. The bare number would be read as a quantity;
    /// the rulebook's own word for the rung is the thing a player is choosing between, and it is
    /// already on screen for everybody else.
    /// </summary>
    private string ValueText => Word is { } word ? $"{Rank}d {word}" : $"{Rank}d";

    /// <summary>
    /// The keys a slider is expected to answer to.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Nothing here suppresses the browser's default, and that is not a gap — it is
    /// the wrong layer for it.</b> Blazor fixes an element's <c>preventDefault</c> at render
    /// time rather than per event, so suppressing it here would apply to every key this handler
    /// sees and swallow Tab along with Home and End, trapping focus inside a rank row — much
    /// worse than what it would fix. Left and Right need no such thing: the pips are
    /// horizontal, and this app is held to no horizontal overflow by a harness in CI, so those
    /// two scroll nothing regardless.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Home and End still reach the browser's own scrolling, which is what
    /// <see cref="Sliders"/> exists to stop — a native listener on <see cref="_pips"/> that
    /// answers to exactly those two keys and nothing else, registered once the element is on
    /// the page. See <see cref="OnAfterRenderAsync"/>.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task Key(KeyboardEventArgs e) => e.Key switch
    {
        "ArrowRight" or "ArrowUp" => Set(Rank + 1),
        "ArrowLeft" or "ArrowDown" => Set(Rank - 1),
        "Home" => Set(Min),
        "End" => Set(Ceiling),
        _ => Task.CompletedTask,
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The one place a rank is bounded, so the announced bounds and the enforced ones cannot
    /// disagree. <see cref="Ceiling"/> rather than <see cref="Max"/>, and the ordering matters:
    /// <c>Math.Clamp</c> throws when its low bound is above its high one, which a house Trait Cap
    /// below a package's granted rank really does produce.
    /// </summary>
    private Task Set(int value) =>
        RankChanged.InvokeAsync(Math.Clamp(value, Min, Ceiling));

    /// <summary>The slider element, guarded against Home/End on first render. See <see cref="Sliders"/>.</summary>
    private ElementReference _pips;

    /// <summary>
    /// Attaches the Home/End guard once, when the element first exists.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Once per component instance, not once per render.</b> Blazor keeps the same
    /// DOM node across re-renders of one row — a rank changing does not recreate the element —
    /// so <paramref name="firstRender"/> is the whole of what is needed; the script's own
    /// idempotence in <see cref="Sliders.Guard"/> is the second line of defence, not the
    /// first.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender) await Sliders.Guard(_pips);
    }
}
